    "body": "## What happened\n\nCreating a sub-issue under #104 fired two separate GitHub webhook deliveries within the same second:\n\n- `sub_issues` / `action: sub_issue_added` on the parent issue\n- `sub_issues` / `action: parent_issue_added` on the child issue\n\nBoth reached staging and were pulled by the bridge, but `topics.py` doesn't recognize the `sub_issues` event at all today \u2014 same bucket as `check_run`/`push`, which `test_check_run_and_push_match_nothing_yet` already documents as a deliberate, known gap. So right now these are silently dropped, which is fine as far as it goes.\n\n## What's needed before `sub_issues` gets wired up\n\nWhen `sub_issues` does get a topic, the two-delivery shape needs a decision, not a default: `sub_issue_added` and `parent_issue_added` are two different GitHub events for what a human reading #104 would call *one* action (linking a sub-issue). Delivered to the same subscriber inside the same poll and matched to the same topic, #106's existing per-topic batching in `_fan_out_batch` should already collapse them into one digest \u2014 but that's untested for this specific pair, and topics.py has no mapping for either event yet to exercise it against.\n\nScope: add `sub_issues` (`sub_issue_added`/`parent_issue_added`) to `topics.py`, and add a fixture-driven test (real payloads are already captured in `cloud/tests/fixtures/github_webhooks/`, or can be recaptured from #104's own delivery history) confirming both events on one linking action collapse to a single notification rather than two.",
